<section class="reference-section" id="indra-vritra"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Indra and Vritra · The Release of the Waters</h2><a href="/indra-vritra.html">Return to story</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p><strong>Rigveda 1.32 · Dr Ganga Sahay Sharma’s Hindi edition, based on Sayana’s commentary.</strong> This original English reader follows the owner-supplied volume and does not reproduce its translation. The private PDF is not distributed by this website.</p><ol><li><strong>1.32.1–2 · supplied PDF pp. 204–205</strong><small>Indra’s deeds, the striking of the serpent-like restrainer, the opening of mountain passages, Tvashtar’s fashioning of the vajra and the waters moving onward.</small></li><li><strong>1.32.3–5 · supplied PDF pp. 204–205</strong><small>Soma, the chosen vajra, Vritra’s defeat, the appearance of sun, dawn and sky, and the fallen-tree comparison.</small></li><li><strong>1.32.6–9 · supplied PDF p. 205</strong><small>Vritra’s resistance, the blow to his body, waters moving beyond him and the image of Danu beside her son.</small></li><li><strong>1.32.10–11 · supplied PDF pp. 205–206</strong><small>The body amid the waters and the opening of courses previously confined like cattle behind an enclosure.</small></li><li><strong>1.32.12–13 · supplied PDF p. 206</strong><small>The counterstroke and the failure of thunder, lightning, mist and hail to reverse the outcome.</small></li><li><strong>1.32.14–15 · supplied PDF p. 206</strong><small>The question about Indra’s fear and swift crossing, followed by the hymn’s closing praise of his sovereignty.</small></li></ol><p><strong>Editorial boundary.</strong> The hymn is praise-poetry, not continuous prose. The page groups its images into a readable sequence while keeping the late question about fear and the final widening into sovereignty. Later Puranic accounts of Vritrasura are separate and are not merged into this Rigvedic presentation. “Vritra” is explained through the hymn’s role of restraining or enclosing the waters; no claim is made that every occurrence elsewhere carries one identical meaning.</p><p>All three images are newly AI-generated interpretations used once each. Indra is shown with two arms and one vajra; Vritra is represented in serpent-like form. Figures, clothing, weapon design, mountains and river courses are imagined. The artwork is devotional/editorial interpretation, not a historical reconstruction or manuscript reproduction.</p></div></section>
